---
import Landing from '../../layouts/Landing.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Landing
  title="Introducing TalesMUD — TalesMUD Blog"
  description="What TalesMUD is and why it exists. Bringing MUDs into the modern web era."
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="blog-post-main">
    <article class="blog-article">
      <div class="blog-article-header">
        <div class="blog-article-meta">
          <span class="blog-post-date">December 1, 2024</span>
          <span class="blog-post-sep">·</span>
          <span class="blog-post-readtime">8 min read</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="blog-article-title">
          Introducing TalesMUD — A Modern Framework for Browser-Based MUDs
        </h1>
        <p class="blog-article-subtitle">
          Bringing the classic Multi-User Dungeon format into the modern web era.
        </p>
        <div class="blog-post-tags">
          <span class="blog-tag">announcement</span>
          <span class="blog-tag">framework</span>
          <span class="blog-tag">open-source</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="blog-article-body">
        <p>
          If you grew up in the late 80s or 90s, you might remember MUDs — Multi-User Dungeons.
          Text-based multiplayer RPGs where you'd explore procedurally described rooms, fight monsters
          with commands like <code>attack goblin</code>, and chat with other players over a 28.8k modem
          through a bare telnet connection.
        </p>
        <p>
          MUDs pioneered online multiplayer gaming. They had quest systems, character classes, guilds,
          crafting, PvP, and open worlds — years before graphical MMOs existed. And yet today, most
          MUD frameworks are legacy codebases from the 90s, requiring telnet clients, running on
          arcane server software, and completely inaccessible to modern players.
        </p>

        <h2>Why TalesMUD?</h2>
        <p>
          I built TalesMUD because I wanted to play a MUD in my browser. No telnet. No special client.
          Just open a tab, connect via WebSocket, and be somewhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          But more than that, I wanted a framework where I could build a world quickly — with real
          tooling, a content editor, a quest system that actually works, and code I could understand.
          Not a pile of 30-year-old C that requires patches just to compile.
        </p>

        <h2>What TalesMUD Is</h2>
        <p>
          TalesMUD is a complete framework for building browser-based MUDs. It ships with:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A Go backend</strong> — fast, single-binary, embedded Svelte frontend, SQLite storage</li>
          <li><strong>A Svelte game client</strong> — xterm.js terminal rendering, minimap, tab widgets</li>
          <li><strong>A web content editor</strong> — CRUD for rooms, NPCs, items, quests, scripts</li>
          <li><strong>Turn-based combat</strong> — initiative, skills, spells, status effects, loot</li>
          <li><strong>A quest system</strong> — kill, collect, deliver, visit, talk, and custom Lua objectives</li>
          <li><strong>Lua scripting</strong> — extend anything with scripts that have access to 8 game API modules</li>
          <li><strong>Auth0 integration</strong> — optional authentication with role-based access (player, creator, admin)</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Vision: Browser-Native MUDs</h2>
        <p>
          The browser is the universal runtime. Every device has one. The best multiplayer games of the
          next decade will be web-first — not because of technical necessity, but because accessibility
          matters. If players have to install something, you lose most of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          TalesMUD takes the MUD format — which is fundamentally text-based and works beautifully at low
          bandwidth — and makes it entirely browser-native. Players connect via WebSocket. The game client
          is a Svelte app. The server is a statically-linked Go binary with embedded assets that you can
          run anywhere Docker runs.
        </p>

        <h2>Veilspan: The Reference World</h2>
        <p>
          To prove the concept, I built <a href="https://veilspan.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Veilspan</a> —
          a fantasy world set in the ruins of Aethermoor. It's the official demo deployment of TalesMUD,
          running live at <code>veilspan.com</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Veilspan has real zones, real NPCs, real quests, real combat. It's not a tech demo — it's a
          playable game that happens to document every feature of the framework in production.
          If you want to see what TalesMUD can do, go play it.
        </p>

        <h2>What's Next</h2>
        <p>
          TalesMUD is early — version 0.1. There's a lot to build:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Party system (grouping and group combat)</li>
          <li>Crafting system</li>
          <li>More NPC AI states</li>
          <li>World map visualization improvements</li>
          <li>Better mobile UI</li>
          <li>More scripting event hooks</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          But the foundation is solid. The architecture is clean. The content pipeline works.
          And Veilspan is the living proof.
        </p>

        <h2>Get Involved</h2>
        <p>
          TalesMUD is fully open source under the MIT license.
          The code is at <a href="https://github.com/TalesMUD/talesmud" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/TalesMUD/talesmud</a>.
          If you're interested in building text-based worlds, I'd love to have you contribute.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether that's opening issues, fixing bugs, writing docs, or building your own world and
          showing it off — all of it helps grow the ecosystem.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start with the <a href="/docs/getting-started/introduction">Getting Started guide</a>,
          then go <a href="https://veilspan.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">play Veilspan</a> to see what's possible.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="blog-article-footer">
        <a href="/blog" class="btn btn-secondary" style="display: inline-flex;">← All Posts</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Landing>

<style>
  .blog-post-main {
    min-height: calc(100vh - 64px);
    padding: 3rem 2rem 5rem;
  }

  .blog-article {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .blog-article-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .blog-article-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .blog-post-date,
  .blog-post-readtime {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--color-text-dim);
  }

  .blog-post-sep {
    color: var(--color-border);
  }

  .blog-article-title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .blog-article-subtitle {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .blog-post-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .blog-tag {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    padding: 2px 8px;
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.2);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }

  .blog-article-body {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .blog-article-body p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .blog-article-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    color: var(--color-amber);
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(245, 166, 35, 0.2);
  }

  .blog-article-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .blog-article-body li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .blog-article-body a {
    color: var(--color-teal);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .blog-article-body a:hover {
    color: var(--color-amber);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .blog-article-body code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.1);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }

  .blog-article-body strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .blog-article-footer {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
</style>
